# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""OA-size scenario stories, batch 8: prefix sum (rest), binary search, hashing, next-greater. 250+ words each."""

QUESTIONS = [

{"id": "sc-114", "v": "ps-diff", "dom": "shipyard", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Berth Charges of Calder Dock",
 "story": "The great shipyard at Calder Dock has a long row of numbered berths, each fitted with a pump that must be primed before a hull is floated. Berth after berth, the harbour master, a brisk woman named Isolde Marchetti, keeps a ledger in which every berth begins with a fee of zero shillings. All through the season, the dock office receives work orders, and each order has the same form: it names a first berth and a last berth, and it instructs the clerk to add a surcharge of some number of shillings to every berth from the first through the last, both ends included. Surcharges may be positive, for extra caulking, or negative, for a rebate granted to long-standing customers.\n\nThere are an enormous number of such orders, perhaps a million in a busy season, and many of them cover wide stretches of the row. The curious thing about the dock office is that nobody ever asks what any berth owes while the orders are still arriving. The accountants will look at the books exactly once, after the final order of the season has been filed, and will then want the fee standing at every berth, from the first to the last, in a single list.\n\nIsolde's previous clerk used to walk the row of berths for every order, adding the surcharge one berth at a time, and by midsummer he was a pale shadow, still writing figures at midnight. Isolde would like something faster. She also needs to be reminded that the new tug, the Marigold, arrives on Tuesday and that the pilot has asked for a berth with a view of the lighthouse.\n\nIn the canteen the principal talk of the season is the great crab sandwich scandal, which concerned the contents of a lunchbox and need not be reported here.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of berths, and M, the number of work orders. Each of the next M lines gives L, R and V: the first berth, the last berth and the surcharge. Output: N integers, the final fee at each berth, in order.\n\nConstraints: N and M up to 10^6; V between -10^4 and 10^4. Walking through each order's berths one by one is far too slow.",
 "clues": ["add a surcharge of some number of shillings to every berth from the first through the last", "nobody ever asks what any berth owes while the orders are still arriving", "the fee standing at every berth, from the first to the last, in a single list", "Walking through each order's berths one by one is far too slow"],
 "decoys": ["the new tug, the Marigold, arrives on Tuesday", "the great crab sandwich scandal, which concerned the contents of a lunchbox"],
 "core": "Many orders each add a number to a whole range of positions; only the final values at every position are wanted.",
 "gloss": [("caulking", "sealing the gaps between a ship's planks", True), ("surcharge", "an extra fee", True), ("rebate", "a partial refund", True), ("berth", "a place where a ship is moored", True), ("shillings", "an old coin, twelve pence", True)],
 "why": {"ps-range": "That answers range-total questions on fixed data; here the data is what is being changed, in ranges, and read only once at the end."},
 "tell": "Many range additions and a single final read: record the change at the start and the opposite change just past the end, then total up once."},

{"id": "sc-115", "v": "ps-suffix", "dom": "tea terraces", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Monsoon Terraces of Darjhal",
 "story": "The tea estates of Darjhal climb the hillside in narrow terraces, each terrace a flat step cut from the slope and faced with a low retaining wall. The estate manager, a patient man named Raghunath Iyer, has recorded the height of every retaining wall, from the westernmost terrace to the easternmost, in a single row of figures. When the monsoon breaks and rain falls evenly on the whole estate, a terrace holds water in a pool above its wall only if there is a taller wall somewhere to its west AND a taller wall somewhere to its east; the pool then rises until it reaches the lower of the two tallest walls on either side, beyond which it spills away.\n\nRaghunath wishes to know, for the entire estate, the total volume of water that will lie in pools after a monsoon, measured in units where a pool one terrace wide and one unit deep holds one unit. Terraces at the very edges of the row can hold nothing, as there is nothing taller beyond them on one side. He is not interested in which terraces flood or how deep; only the grand total.\n\nThe estate's cook is notorious for adding far too much cardamom to the afternoon chai, and the pickers have taken to bringing their own flasks. A pair of sulky macaques steals oranges from the manager's veranda. Raghunath insists on holding a short ceremony each year in which the first leaf of the season is plucked by the youngest picker, and in the photograph she always blinks.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of terraces. Line two gives N wall heights, west to east. Output: the total volume of water held after the monsoon.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; heights up to 10^5. Raghunath's first attempt looked outward from each terrace in both directions to find the tallest wall on each side, and by the time it reached the hundredth thousand terrace he had retired for the day. He would prefer a method that passes along the row a small, fixed number of times.",
 "clues": ["a taller wall somewhere to its west AND a taller wall somewhere to its east", "the lower of the two tallest walls on either side", "looked outward from each terrace in both directions to find the tallest wall on each side", "passes along the row a small, fixed number of times"],
 "decoys": ["The estate's cook is notorious for adding far too much cardamom to the afternoon chai", "A pair of sulky macaques steals oranges from the manager's veranda"],
 "core": "For each position, you need the tallest value anywhere to its left and the tallest anywhere to its right; combine them and add up.",
 "gloss": [("terrace", "a flat step cut into a hillside", True), ("monsoon", "a season of heavy seasonal rain", True), ("retaining wall", "a wall that holds back earth or water", True), ("spills", "overflows", False), ("estate", "a large area of land under one owner", True)],
 "why": {"ms-hist": "A strictly ordered pass with a monotone structure also works, but the question as posed is solved most naturally by two sweeps, one from each side.", "hs-freq": "Counting does not capture 'taller somewhere to the west'."},
 "tell": "Each answer needs information from everything on the left and everything on the right: build both running summaries by sweeping from each end."},

{"id": "sc-116", "v": "bs-exact", "dom": "seismology", "diff": "E",
 "title": "The Tremor Archive at Cragmoor",
 "story": "The seismological observatory at Cragmoor has been logging ground tremors since the reign of Queen Victoria, and its archive is one of the largest in the country. Each recorded event has been assigned a serial number at the time of logging, and because the clerks in those days numbered strictly upward and never reused a number, the archive's index card, which lists every serial number held, is arranged in increasing order from the oldest event to the newest. No serial number appears twice on the index card.\n\nThe present archivist, Dr Matilde Ferreira, receives requests from researchers around the world. A typical request reads: 'does your archive hold serial number 48,120,331, and if so, at what position on your index card?' She answers one such request at a time. The index card has been digitised into a long list with several tens of millions of entries, so that scanning it entry by entry would take longer than the researchers are prepared to wait. She also does not want to copy the list or rearrange it, since the original ordering is itself part of the archive's heritage.\n\nA digression about the observatory: the building is said to be haunted by the ghost of a nineteenth-century geologist who was killed when his instruments fell off the shelf during an earthquake that he was in the midst of measuring. The night watchman has reported cold spots on the upper landing. There is also a gift shop, which sells mugs.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of serial numbers on the card, and Q, the number of requests. Line two gives the N serial numbers in increasing order. Each of the next Q lines gives one requested serial number. Output: for every request, the 1-based position on the card, or -1 if the number is absent.\n\nConstraints: N up to 5 * 10^7; Q up to 10^5; serial numbers up to 10^18. Each request should be answered in a number of steps that grows only very slowly as the card gets longer.",
 "clues": ["arranged in increasing order from the oldest event to the newest", "No serial number appears twice on the index card", "scanning it entry by entry would take longer than the researchers are prepared to wait", "a number of steps that grows only very slowly as the card gets longer"],
 "decoys": ["the building is said to be haunted by the ghost of a nineteenth-century geologist", "There is also a gift shop, which sells mugs"],
 "core": "A huge sorted list of distinct values; for each request report where the value is or that it is missing.",
 "gloss": [("seismological", "relating to the study of earthquakes", True), ("digitised", "converted into computer form", True), ("archivist", "someone who keeps records", True), ("heritage", "something valued that is passed down", True), ("serial number", "a number identifying an item in a series", True)],
 "why": {"bs-bound": "A boundary search is for 'first position at or past' a value; here an exact match or its absence is requested, and values do not repeat.", "hs-seen": "A lookup table would answer too, but it would mean copying tens of millions of entries, which the archivist refuses."},
 "tell": "A sorted list, a value to find, and a step count that must grow only slowly: halve the stretch you are looking in each time."},

{"id": "sc-117", "v": "bs-bound", "dom": "cable railways", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Timetable of the Hohenfels Funicular",
 "story": "The funicular at Hohenfels climbs a precipitous mountainside from the spa town in the valley to a hotel on the ridge, and it is one of the oldest of its kind in the Alps. Each day the cars depart from the lower station according to a fixed timetable, and the conductor, Ferdinand Brandl, has the departure times printed on a long scroll. Times are given in minutes after midnight, and, because the funicular runs more often in the busy hours, several departures can share the same minute, one on each of its two tracks, so the timetable contains repeats.\n\nTourists constantly wander into the ticket office and ask the same sort of question: 'I have just arrived at the lower station at minute T; when is the very next departure that I can catch?' A departure at exactly minute T can be caught. Ferdinand needs to answer instantly, and not merely for one tourist but for hundreds each hour, each with a different T. The scroll is read from start to finish, in order of time, and it is far too long to scan from the beginning every time. For each tourist the conductor wants to know the position, counted along the scroll, of the earliest departure at or after minute T, so that he can then read off the actual time. If the tourist has arrived after the very last departure of the day, he should be told that the day's service is over.\n\nThe hotel at the top is famous for its apple strudel, which Ferdinand eats on his Sundays off. The funicular's cars are painted a cheerful yellow and have been repainted so many times that the doors no longer shut flush. A newly arrived party of schoolchildren has been singing in the waiting room for a quarter of an hour.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of departures, and Q, the number of tourists. Line two gives the N departure times in non-decreasing order. Each of the next Q lines gives one arrival minute T. Output: for each tourist, the 1-based position of the first departure at or after T, or 0 if none.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; Q up to 10^6; times up to 1440. Each answer must be found without examining most of the scroll.",
 "clues": ["several departures can share the same minute, one on each of its two tracks, so the timetable contains repeats", "the earliest departure at or after minute T", "the position, counted along the scroll"],
 "decoys": ["The hotel at the top is famous for its apple strudel", "The funicular's cars are painted a cheerful yellow"],
 "core": "In a sorted list with repeated values, find the first position whose value is at least T.",
 "gloss": [("funicular", "a railway pulled up a steep slope by cables", True), ("precipitous", "very steep", True), ("conductor", "an official on a train who checks tickets", True), ("scroll", "a roll of paper used for a long list", True), ("non-decreasing", "never getting smaller, though values may repeat", False)],
 "why": {"bs-exact": "An exact search returns any matching position or reports absence; here a match may be absent yet the answer is still the first position after it.", "tp-merge": "Nothing here is being merged; there is just one sorted list and many separate questions."},
 "tell": "A sorted list with repeats and a question about the first position at or past a threshold: search for the boundary, not the value."},

{"id": "sc-118", "v": "bs-answer", "dom": "mountaineering", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Porters of Kangchul Base Camp",
 "story": "An expedition to the northern flank of Kangchul has established its base camp at nearly five thousand metres, and its logistics officer, a calm and meticulous man named Tenzing Dorje, must now move the season's supplies up the glacier to a higher camp. The supplies are packed in a row of crates, each of a known weight, laid out on the sledge-ground in a fixed order of loading, and for reasons of safety the crates must be carried up in exactly that order: the first porter takes some crates from the start of the row, the next takes the crates that follow, and so on, with no crate skipped or reordered.\n\nThe expedition has hired a fixed number of days, D, for the carry, and each day exactly one party goes up and one party comes back; each party carries a consecutive batch of crates from the row, and every crate must be moved within the D days. A party may carry any number of crates provided the combined weight does not exceed the load limit L that Tenzing sets for the whole expedition; the same limit applies to every day. Tenzing wants to know the smallest load limit L, a whole number of kilograms, with which the entire row of crates can be moved within the D days. A limit so low that some crate alone exceeds it is obviously useless.\n\nThe expedition's cook has discovered that yak butter makes an acceptable substitute for olive oil and has begun to experiment alarmingly with it. The expedition's radio operator has not yet established contact with the valley, but expects to once the weather clears. A rival team on the neighbouring peak has been spotted wearing suspiciously bright jackets.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of crates, and D. Line two gives the N crate weights in loading order. Output: the smallest whole-number load limit L that allows all crates to be moved in at most D days.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^5; each weight up to 10^4; D between 1 and N. Tenzing cannot afford to test every possible load limit one after another, but he has noticed that once a limit works, any larger limit works too; and once a limit fails, any smaller limit fails.",
 "clues": ["the smallest load limit L, a whole number of kilograms, with which the entire row of crates can be moved", "the crates must be carried up in exactly that order", "once a limit works, any larger limit works too; and once a limit fails, any smaller limit fails"],
 "decoys": ["The expedition's cook has discovered that yak butter makes an acceptable substitute for olive oil", "A rival team on the neighbouring peak has been spotted wearing suspiciously bright jackets"],
 "core": "Find the smallest limit that lets an ordered row be split into at most D consecutive batches; any larger limit would also work.",
 "gloss": [("logistics", "organising the movement of supplies", True), ("meticulous", "very careful about detail", True), ("flank", "the side of a mountain", True), ("consecutive", "following one right after another", False), ("combined", "added together", True)],
 "why": {"dp-interval": "Splitting a sequence into pieces sounds like range dynamic programming, but the question asks for a single smallest limit that is checked for feasibility, a pass-or-fail test.", "gr-local": "Filling each day as full as possible is only the check for one proposed limit, not the search for the best limit."},
 "tell": "'Smallest value that works' plus 'if it works, bigger works': search the answer itself, testing each guess with a quick pass."},

{"id": "sc-119", "v": "bs-rotated", "dom": "perfumery", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Carousel of Maison Vetiver",
 "story": "In the atelier of Maison Vetiver, in the old quarter of Grasse, the master perfumer keeps her essences on a great circular carousel that turns slowly on a brass spindle. Each essence is stored in a numbered bottle, and the number reflects its potency, with every potency different from every other. Originally the bottles were placed on the carousel in increasing order of potency, starting from the weakest and running around to the strongest. But over the decades the carousel has been turned by apprentices, and its starting mark has drifted: the line of bottles now begins somewhere in the middle of the original sequence, runs up to the strongest, and then continues from the weakest up to the bottle that originally sat just before the new start. No one knows how far it has turned.\n\nThe perfumer's assistant, a nervous young man named Basile, is sent to find the position of a particular essence, identified by its potency number, among the bottles as they currently stand along the shelf in front of the carousel. The shelf is read from its present first bottle to its last. There are many thousands of bottles, since the Maison has acquired the entire collection of a rival house, and checking them one by one would take Basile all afternoon. He knows only that the potency numbers are all different, and that the arrangement is a cut-and-turned version of a sorted row, as described.\n\nOutside, the lavender fields are in bloom and the tourists have gathered at the roadside in their wide hats. A delivery of orange blossom from the south has been delayed by a landslide. The perfumer has a pet parrot that can imitate the sound of a cork being drawn.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of bottles, and the target potency P. Line two gives the N potency numbers as the shelf now reads. Output: the 1-based position of the bottle with potency P, or -1 if there is none.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; potency numbers are distinct and up to 10^9. The shelf is not to be sorted again or copied, and the number of bottles inspected should grow only slowly with N.",
 "clues": ["Originally the bottles were placed on the carousel in increasing order of potency", "the line of bottles now begins somewhere in the middle of the original sequence", "every potency different from every other", "a cut-and-turned version of a sorted row"],
 "decoys": ["The perfumer has a pet parrot that can imitate the sound of a cork being drawn", "A delivery of orange blossom from the south has been delayed by a landslide"],
 "core": "A sorted row of distinct values has been rotated to start at an unknown point; find a given value without scanning everything.",
 "gloss": [("atelier", "a workshop of an artist or craftsperson", True), ("essence", "a concentrated fragrant extract", True), ("potency", "strength", True), ("carousel", "a rotating platform", True), ("spindle", "a rod on which something turns", True)],
 "why": {"bs-exact": "The classic search needs a fully sorted row; here at every halving at least one half is still sorted, and you must work out which.", "bs-bound": "No boundary of values is asked for, just a specific bottle."},
 "tell": "Sorted but rotated: at each halving, one side is still in order, so decide whether the target lies inside it."},

{"id": "sc-120", "v": "bs-partition", "dom": "choral music", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Middle Voice of the Combined Choirs",
 "story": "The town of Brindlecombe has two choirs, the Cathedral Singers and the Guild Chorale, and for the bicentenary celebrations they have agreed to perform together in a single massed choir. The conductor, a formidable woman named Perpetua Ashgrove, has asked each choir's secretary to list the pitch of every singer, using a numerical scale in which a higher number means a higher voice. Each secretary has, helpfully, listed their singers in ascending order of pitch, so the Singers' list and the Chorale's list are each sorted already. The two lists have different lengths.\n\nPerpetua's request is peculiar: she wants to know the median pitch of the combined choir, that is, the pitch of the singer who would stand exactly in the middle if every singer from both choirs were lined up in order of pitch, or, if the combined choir has an even number of singers, the average of the two middle pitches. She does not want the lists merged, because the choirs are proud and the secretaries would feel their work disparaged if their tidy lists were simply poured together; besides, the choirs run to many thousands of singers because of a recent festival, and merging them all just to read a middle value feels wasteful. The task must be done in a number of steps that grows only with the logarithm of the size of the smaller list.\n\nBrindlecombe is otherwise famous for a cheese festival at which a wheel of cheddar is rolled down a hill, and the winning team is whoever catches it. The cathedral organ is currently being cleaned, and the choir practises with a battered upright piano. Perpetua is allergic to dust.\n\nInput: line one gives A and B, the sizes of the two lists. Line two gives A pitches in ascending order; line three gives B pitches in ascending order. Output: the median pitch of all A + B singers, to one decimal place.\n\nConstraints: A and B up to 10^6, at least one of them nonzero; pitches up to 10^9. Producing a third merged list, or sorting everything again, is not permitted.",
 "clues": ["each choir's secretary to list the pitch of every singer", "the Singers' list and the Chorale's list are each sorted already", "the median pitch of the combined choir", "She does not want the lists merged", "grows only with the logarithm of the size of the smaller list"],
 "decoys": ["a cheese festival at which a wheel of cheddar is rolled down a hill", "The cathedral organ is currently being cleaned"],
 "core": "Find the median of two sorted lists together, without merging them, in logarithmic time.",
 "gloss": [("bicentenary", "a two-hundredth anniversary", True), ("median", "the middle value when everything is in order", False), ("disparaged", "spoken of as having little value", True), ("formidable", "inspiring respect or fear", True), ("massed", "gathered together in a large group", True)],
 "why": {"tp-merge": "Walking both lists together would find the middle, but takes time proportional to the sizes, whereas the story demands a logarithm of the smaller list.", "hp-two": "Two balanced halves are a streaming idea; here the lists are already sorted and fixed."},
 "tell": "Median of two sorted lists in logarithmic time: search for how many items of the smaller list belong on the left side of the cut."},

{"id": "sc-121", "v": "hs-seen", "dom": "customs house", "diff": "E",
 "title": "The Counterfeit Seals of Port Verrin",
 "story": "At the customs house of Port Verrin, every crate that clears the quay is stamped with a wax seal bearing a serial number, and the seal is copied into the day's log by the junior clerk on duty. The numbers are not meant to repeat: each seal is struck from a unique die, and the die is broken after a single use so that no two crates can ever carry the same serial. The collector of customs, a suspicious man named Lucan Oakshott, has long suspected that smugglers are forging seals, and if so, a forged seal would eventually be copied into the log with the same serial as a genuine one already recorded.\n\nLucan has asked his deputy to examine the log, which is a long list of serial numbers in the order the crates cleared the quay, and to identify the first moment at which a serial number appears that has been seen earlier in the log. That is, scanning the log from the start, the deputy must report the position of the first entry whose serial number is equal to some entry before it. If no serial number is ever repeated, the log is clean and the deputy should say so. The deputy does not need to know which earlier entry was matched or how many duplicates exist; only the first offending position.\n\nPort Verrin is famous for a variety of pickled herring that no visitor can stomach, though the locals eat it with every meal. The quay has a resident colony of pigeons that has defeated three successive attempts at removal. The collector's wife keeps a stall of embroidered handkerchiefs near the toll gate and is rumoured to be the true power in the port.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of log entries. Line two gives N serial numbers in the order the crates cleared the quay. Output: the 1-based position of the first entry whose serial number appeared earlier in the log, or 0 if all are distinct.\n\nConstraints: N up to 2 * 10^6; serial numbers up to 10^18. The log is in no particular order and must not be sorted, because the order of clearance is itself evidence. The deputy would like to finish in a single pass.",
 "clues": ["the first moment at which a serial number appears that has been seen earlier in the log", "the position of the first entry whose serial number is equal to some entry before it", "must not be sorted, because the order of clearance is itself evidence", "in a single pass"],
 "decoys": ["a variety of pickled herring that no visitor can stomach", "The collector's wife keeps a stall of embroidered handkerchiefs near the toll gate"],
 "core": "Scan a list in order and report the first entry that has already appeared before.",
 "gloss": [("customs", "the authority that inspects goods crossing a border", True), ("quay", "a landing place for ships", True), ("forging", "making a fake copy", True), ("die", "a tool that stamps a pattern", True), ("clean", "free of repeats", False)],
 "why": {"hs-freq": "A full count of every serial is more than is needed; only whether this one has been met before.", "ps-count": "No totals are involved."},
 "tell": "Going through in order and asking 'have I met this value already?': remember what has been seen in a set that answers instantly."},

{"id": "sc-122", "v": "hs-freq", "dom": "letterpress printing", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Compositor's Case",
 "story": "In the cellar of a print shop in the old town of Ostrava stands a wooden type case, the last of its kind still in daily use, and its compositor, a stooped and cheerful man named Vaclav Hrdina, sets every poster and pamphlet by hand. The case holds individual pieces of metal type, called sorts, each bearing one raised letter, and the shop owns only so many of each letter. Vaclav's trade secret, which he reveals to no one but his apprentice, is that he counts the sorts of each letter at the start of every day and writes the tallies on a slate beside the case.\n\nThis morning a customer has brought in a handwritten message, a single long line of letters and spaces, which he wants printed on a banner for his daughter's wedding. Vaclav must decide whether the shop's stock of sorts can set the message at all. Each letter of the message needs one sort of that letter, used once and not returned to the case until the printing is done, and spaces do not need sorts. If the message needs, say, five of the letter 'e' but the case holds only four, then the banner cannot be set, no matter how much stock of other letters remains. Vaclav does not care in what order the letters appear in the message, nor where the sorts are physically stored, only whether, letter by letter, supply meets demand.\n\nThe print shop also publishes a small gazette of parish news, which is chiefly read by people who appear in it. The cellar is damp and smells faintly of linseed oil. A feud with the printer next door over the exact placement of a shared drainpipe has simmered for eleven years.\n\nInput: line one gives the length N of the customer's message and the total number M of sorts in the case. Line two gives the message as a string of N characters. Line three gives the case contents as a string of M letters, one per sort. Output: YES if the message can be set, NO otherwise.\n\nConstraints: N and M up to 10^6; letters are lowercase a to z and spaces appear only in the message. Do not try to match letters by searching the case afresh for each letter of the message; Vaclav wants a method that scales with the lengths of the two strings, not with their product.",
 "clues": ["Each letter of the message needs one sort of that letter, used once", "letter by letter, supply meets demand", "does not care in what order the letters appear in the message", "scales with the lengths of the two strings, not with their product"],
 "decoys": ["a small gazette of parish news, which is chiefly read by people who appear in it", "A feud with the printer next door over the exact placement of a shared drainpipe"],
 "core": "Decide whether a message can be built from a pool of letters: for each letter, is there enough supply?",
 "gloss": [("compositor", "a person who arranges type for printing", True), ("sorts", "individual pieces of metal type", True), ("tallies", "running counts", True), ("stooped", "bent forward at the shoulders", True), ("supply", "the amount available", False)],
 "why": {"hs-seen": "Whether a letter exists in the case is not enough; how many are needed matters.", "hs-group": "Nothing here is being gathered into groups."},
 "tell": "'How many of each' on both sides, compared kind by kind: tally each side in a table that maps each letter to a count."},

{"id": "sc-123", "v": "hs-index", "dom": "tulip auction", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Budget of Mynheer van der Linden",
 "story": "In the great auction hall at Haarlem, the tulip season brings merchants from across Europe, and among them Mynheer Willem van der Linden, whose estate in the countryside has an enormous conservatory and an insatiable appetite for rare bulbs. This year he has set his steward a task. The auctioneer has published a catalogue of lots, each lot being a single bulb offered at a stated price in guilders, and the lots are printed in the order in which they will come under the hammer. Prices are all whole numbers, and the same price can well appear for two different lots.\n\nVan der Linden's steward must choose exactly two different lots whose prices add up to precisely the sum that Van der Linden has set aside, no more and no less, since he distrusts debts and refuses to carry cash home. The steward need not find all such pairs; any one will do. But because Van der Linden will be at the back of the hall, and must be told which two lots to raise a finger for, the steward must report their positions in the catalogue, counted as they are printed, and not the prices. The catalogue is NOT in order of price; it is printed in order of sale, which was decided by lot.\n\nThe auction hall is celebrated for a particularly ornate chandelier, which was installed after a fire and is said to be insured for more than the building. The auctioneer's assistant, a young man with a spectacular moustache, has been known to wave his gavel at the wrong moment. Outside, the canal is frozen and a boy is skating in widening circles.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of lots, and the budget T. Line two gives the N prices in catalogue order. Output: two positions (1-based, smaller first) of lots whose prices sum to T, or the word NONE.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; prices and T up to 10^9; there may be several valid pairs and any is accepted. Reordering the catalogue would lose the positions that the steward is asked to report, and examining every pair of lots is hopeless at this size; he wants to settle each lot in a constant amount of effort as he reads down the catalogue once.",
 "clues": ["exactly two different lots whose prices add up to precisely the sum", "the steward must report their positions in the catalogue", "The catalogue is NOT in order of price", "settle each lot in a constant amount of effort as he reads down the catalogue once"],
 "decoys": ["a particularly ornate chandelier, which was installed after a fire", "The auctioneer's assistant, a young man with a spectacular moustache"],
 "core": "Find two entries in an unsorted list that add to a target, and report their original positions in a single pass.",
 "gloss": [("conservatory", "a glass room for growing plants", True), ("bulb", "the underground storage part of a plant like a tulip", True), ("guilders", "the old Dutch currency", True), ("steward", "someone who manages another person's affairs", True), ("insatiable", "impossible to satisfy", True)],
 "why": {"tp-ends": "Closing in from both ends needs sorted data, but sorting would destroy the positions the steward must report."},
 "alt": [],
 "tell": "Unsorted, a pair that totals a target, original positions needed: remember each value's position and ask what partner would complete the total."},

{"id": "sc-124", "v": "hs-group", "dom": "calligraphy", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Practice Words of Master Kuan",
 "story": "Master Kuan Li runs a school of calligraphy in a lantern-lit alley of an old river city, and the first lesson he gives every new pupil is that a word is a pattern of strokes, and that rearranging the strokes can produce a quite different word. To drive the point home, he writes a long list of practice words on a scroll, all in lower-case Latin letters for the benefit of the foreign students, and asks his senior pupil, Mei, to sort the list into families.\n\nA family, by Kuan's definition, is a set of words that are rearrangements of one another: they contain exactly the same letters, each letter appearing the same number of times, though possibly in a different order. For example, if the words 'listen' and 'silent' appear, they belong to the same family. A word that is not a rearrangement of any other forms a family of one. Mei is to present the families on a fresh scroll, each family on its own line, in any order, and the words inside each family in the order they appeared on the original scroll. The master is not at all interested in the meanings of the words, or whether they belong to any language; only in whether two words are made of the same letters.\n\nKuan's inkstone is said to have belonged to a minor poet of the Tang dynasty, and a brisk trade in forgeries has sprung up among the antique dealers. The pupils' tea is brewed with jasmine. A swallow has made a nest in the eaves above the practice room, and the master forbids anyone to disturb it, with the result that the students have grown accustomed to chirping in the middle of lessons.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of words. The next N lines each give one word. Output: the families, each on its own line, with the words of a family separated by spaces; families may be printed in any order but the words within a family must appear in input order.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^5; each word up to 100 letters. Comparing every pair of words against each other letter by letter would take Mei the whole school year; she needs something that assigns each word to its family in one step, as she reads it.",
 "clues": ["a set of words that are rearrangements of one another", "they contain exactly the same letters, each letter appearing the same number of times", "assigns each word to its family in one step, as she reads it", "Comparing every pair of words against each other letter by letter"],
 "decoys": ["Kuan's inkstone is said to have belonged to a minor poet of the Tang dynasty", "A swallow has made a nest in the eaves above the practice room"],
 "core": "Group words that are made of the same letters; the words are assigned to a family by some form of identity of their letters.",
 "gloss": [("calligraphy", "the art of beautiful handwriting", True), ("strokes", "marks made by a pen or brush", True), ("rearrangements", "the same items in a different order", False), ("forgeries", "fake copies passed off as genuine", True), ("senior", "older or more advanced", True)],
 "why": {"hs-freq": "Counting letters for a single word is only the first step; the question wants the words gathered under a shared label.", "ps-count": "No ranges are being totalled."},
 "tell": "Words belong together if they share some signature: compute a canonical signature for each word and collect words under it."},

{"id": "sc-125", "v": "hs-cyclic", "dom": "cloakroom", "diff": "H",
 "title": "The Lost Tag of the Aldwych Hall",
 "story": "The Aldwych Concert Hall issues a numbered brass tag to every patron who checks a coat, and the tags are numbered consecutively from 1 upward, one per patron, with no skipped numbers on any given evening. Tonight, however, a number of tags have been lost, the tags that remain in the hands of the cloakroom attendant, a retired sergeant named Humphrey Bell, having been dropped by a careless porter into a basket and scrambled. The attendant now holds a jumble of tags in an order that tells him nothing, and the manager has asked him to find the smallest positive tag number that is NOT among those in the basket.\n\nSome tags in the basket are duplicates; the porter, trying to be helpful, ran off copies of a few. Some are zero or negative, being the blanks used for practice, and may be ignored. Some are far larger than the number of tags in the basket, belonging to some enormous cloakroom at the other end of town and to be ignored for the purpose of this calculation. What matters is the smallest positive whole number, starting from 1, that appears nowhere in the basket. For instance, if the basket contained tags numbered 3, 4, -1, 1, the answer would be 2.\n\nThe sergeant is fussy about space. The cloakroom is tiny, there is no spare desk, and he will not allow another basket, another table or a ledger to be brought in: whatever he does must be done by shuffling and marking the tags in the one basket he has, using nothing beyond a few scribbles of his own. He must also be quick; the concert's interval is about to begin, and the first patrons are already queuing.\n\nThe hall's acoustics are praised by visiting orchestras, and its foyer has a marble staircase on which a famous soprano once tripped. The programme tonight includes a long piece for solo viola.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of tags in the basket. Line two gives the N tag numbers, in no order. Output: the smallest positive integer missing.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; tag numbers between -10^9 and 10^9. Extra memory must not grow with N.",
 "clues": ["the smallest positive tag number that is NOT among those in the basket", "shuffling and marking the tags in the one basket he has", "nothing beyond a few scribbles of his own", "numbered consecutively from 1 upward"],
 "decoys": ["a famous soprano once tripped", "The programme tonight includes a long piece for solo viola"],
 "core": "Find the smallest missing positive whole number in an unsorted list, using no extra memory: the list itself must serve as the record.",
 "gloss": [("consecutively", "one after another in order", False), ("sergeant", "a rank of non-commissioned officer", True), ("scrambled", "mixed up", True), ("acoustics", "the qualities that affect how sound is heard in a room", True), ("patron", "a customer or regular supporter", True)],
 "why": {"hs-seen": "A set of seen numbers works, but the story forbids extra memory beyond a few marks.", "bs-exact": "The tags are unordered; sorting them would cost extra effort and still not satisfy the memory limit as stated."},
 "tell": "Values that double as positions, and no extra memory allowed: put each tag at the slot matching its number, then see which slot is wrong."},

{"id": "sc-126", "v": "ms-next", "dom": "signal fires", "diff": "M",
 "title": "The Beacon Ridge of Carrow Fell",
 "story": "Along the spine of Carrow Fell, from the coast to the border, stand the old beacon towers that once carried warning of invasion from one hilltop to the next. They are all different in height, since they were built by different lords at different centuries and from different quarries, and some are only the stumps of what they once were. A local historical society, led by an indefatigable amateur named Cordelia Fenwick, is trying to reconstruct how the fire signals might have passed along the ridge.\n\nCordelia's working theory is simple: from any given tower, the watchman looked eastward along the ridge, and the first tower he could see that was strictly taller than his own was the one to which he would pass the signal. Any tower lower than or equal to his own was hidden behind the shoulder of the hill and could not be seen. If he could see none, because no taller tower stood anywhere to the east, the signal died there. Cordelia has the heights of all the towers listed from west to east. For EVERY tower, she wishes to know the distance, in towers counted eastward, to the nearest taller tower, or zero if there is none.\n\nThe society meets on Thursdays in a draughty village hall, and the tea urn has a tendency to boil over. Cordelia's late husband was a keen kite flier and the hall is decorated with his kites, which is nobody's idea of a safe arrangement. A local farmer insists that the beacon on Carrow Fell was once the site of an alien landing, though he is not taken seriously.\n\nInput: line one gives N, the number of towers. Line two gives N heights, west to east. Output: N integers, the eastward distance from each tower to the nearest strictly taller tower, or 0 if none.\n\nConstraints: N up to 10^6; heights up to 10^9. A method that, for each tower, looks eastward until it finds a taller one, takes far too long when the ridge descends gently from a great peak, because every tower must look past almost every other. Cordelia needs each tower to be settled using only a small amount of average effort.",
 "clues": ["the first tower he could see that was strictly taller than his own", "For EVERY tower, she wishes to know the distance", "for each tower, looks eastward until it finds a taller one, takes far too long"],
 "decoys": ["The society meets on Thursdays in a draughty village hall", "A local farmer insists that the beacon on Carrow Fell was once the site of an alien landing"],
 "core": "For each position, find the nearest later position holding a strictly larger value.",
 "gloss": [("beacon", "a fire or light used as a signal", True), ("indefatigable", "never tiring", True), ("stumps", "short remaining pieces", True), ("strictly", "excluding equal values", False), ("draughty", "full of cold air currents", True)],
 "why": {"ms-hist": "Both use an ordered structure, but histogram areas combine neighbours on both sides; here only the next larger value to one side matters.", "sw-deque": "No frame of fixed length is involved."},
 "tell": "For each element, the nearest larger one to its right: keep unresolved elements in order and resolve them when something bigger arrives."},
]
